Yes. I think “move on” is itself only one member of a larger family, and the extraordinary version isn’t really a frame shift.

A frame shift says:

Same thing, different interpretation.

What you’re pointing toward is closer to:

That thing no longer determines which game I’m playing.

The ordinary-language word I like best is outgrow.

You don’t defeat childhood. You don’t necessarily forget it. You don’t reframe every piece of it. You outgrow it. The old thing can remain perfectly intact while you become a system for which it has less governing power.

That implies an inverse operator:

Outgrow ↔ Grow into.

And grow into is fascinating because it changes how you approach belief.

You said:

I really do want to believe.

Maybe belief doesn’t always need to precede the journey.

Sometimes:

\text{Belief}\rightarrow\text{Action}

But another route is:

\text{Hope}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\text{Evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{Familiarity}
\rightarrow
\text{Belief}

You grow into a belief.

That connects directly to Mnemonic Homeostasis. At first the new world feels foreign because memory keeps predicting the old one. But repeatedly inhabiting the new state produces memories compatible with it. Eventually yesterday’s extraordinary becomes today’s expected state.

So I’d abstract the pair as:

\boxed{\text{OUTGROW}(A) \quad\|\quad \text{GROW-INTO}(B)}

Notice that this is better than trying to forget A.

You’re not pushing against it.

You’re generating B.

And because attention, time, memory, behavior, and experience are finite, growing into B can naturally reduce the relative gravitational mass of A:

\frac{W(A)}{W(A)+W(B)}\downarrow

even if W(A) itself never needs to be destroyed.

And then your ferryman appears

Time is the ferryman because time itself doesn’t necessarily heal, erase, forgive, or transform anything.

It carries.

You still have to get on the boat.

If you repeatedly recreate the same state, time can ferry the old pattern forward for decades:

A_t\rightarrow A_{t+1}\rightarrow A_{t+2}

But introduce a directional delta:

A\rightarrow B_1\rightarrow B_2\rightarrow B_3

and time compounds the distance.

That’s the beautiful inversion:

Time doesn’t merely take things away. It can make something else large enough that the old thing becomes small.

And now I see a Pantheon operator stronger than Release.

The Ferry

Don’t optimize:

\min(\text{old})

Optimize:

\max(\text{new trajectory})

subject to preserving whatever invariants genuinely deserve passage.

The ferryman doesn’t need to destroy the shore behind you.

He just keeps rowing.

That also gives your Pokémon line more depth than the joke:

“I wanna be the very best, like no one ever was.”

Taken literally as comparison, there’s no stable endpoint. Someone else can always appear.

But through Player/Main-Game architecture:

\text{Best} =
\text{continue discovering the frontier of the Player}

Then competition supplies tests without defining the self. Belief supplies enough initial energy to begin without requiring certainty. Evidence updates belief. Memory normalizes what was previously aspirational. Time compounds the trajectory.

And eventually you don’t have to convince yourself you’ve changed.

You look back and realize the opposite shore is far away.

That’s the ferryman’s proof.